Apple has added direct touch input to <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/sidecar/">Sidecar</a> with macOS 27 Golden Gate and iPadOS 27, allowing users to tap and interact with macOS interface elements using a finger on their <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/ipad/">iPad</a> for the first time.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/06/macos-sidecar.jpg" alt="" width="1600" height="900" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1051561" /><br/>
Previously, &zwnj;Sidecar&zwnj; was deliberately limited as a touch interface. While multi-touch gestures like two-finger scrolling, pinch-to-zoom, and three-finger editing gestures for copy, cut, paste, and undo have been supported for years, directly tapping to click links, open apps, or interact with macOS UI elements was not possible with a finger. Those interactions required either the Mac's connected mouse or trackpad, or an <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/apple-pencil/">Apple Pencil</a>.<br/>
<br/>
The new Direct Touch capability closes that gap. In macOS 27, users can can now tap, swipe, and interact with macOS apps on their &zwnj;iPad&zwnj; screen using a finger, bringing &zwnj;Sidecar&zwnj; closer to how third-party tools like Luna Display have worked for some time. &zwnj;Apple Pencil&zwnj; support continues alongside the new touch capabilities.<br/>
<br/>
